WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.100
Welcome back to SpaceX Daily Episode 3, it's June 14th, 2026, and I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:11.580
Let's get into today's SpaceX developments. The week's biggest developments pulled together

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:18.180
what actually moved, why it matters, and what to watch next. SpaceX listed on NASDAQ under the ticker

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:26.360
SPCX on June 12th at $135 per share and closed the first day near $161.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:32.920
Fresh reports now place the company at a $2 trillion valuation driven by progress on Starship

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:39.480
and Orbital Compute plans. The prospectus baseline showed roughly $18.7 billion in projected

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:45.300
2025 revenue, with Starlink accounting for about two-thirds of that total and already profitable.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:51.780
That valuation ultimately depends on execution at scale, and the clearest recent signal of that

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:57.540
scale came from the factory floor. The Gigabay factory received its first public tour this week,

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:03.000
showing tooling and floor space laid out for rapid reuse and orbital refueling operations.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.340
The facility is sized for a production rate of 1,000 starships per year,

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.600
directly addressing the production volume question that has constrained starship flight test

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:19.520
tempo so far. While the factory prepares the fleet, the engine that powers it advanced another step.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.580
Raptor 3 now runs at higher chamber pressure than any previous methalox engine by using a

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:30.700
full-flow, staged combustion cycle that routes all propellants through both turbo pumps.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:36.800
The redesign removes the gas generator and many valves, cutting part count and production time.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:44.540
Thrust exceeds 280 metric tons, while specific impulse stays competitive for both sea level and vacuum

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:49.580
versions. Production rate gains are now the main variable against starship demand.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:56.000
From an engineering standpoint, full-flow staged combustion works by sending every bit of fuel and

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.760
oxidizer through the turbines before they reach the combustion chamber. That single change extracts

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.520
more energy from the propellants, raises pressure, and removes entire subsystems that used to be

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:12.540
needed just to drive the pumps. The result is fewer parts to manufacture, fewer joints that can leak,

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:18.520
and a shorter assembly line, which is exactly why the cycle has become central to hitting the volumes

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:24.220
the factory tour just showed. With engines and ships moving faster, the launch cadence that funds

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:31.480
everything kept climbing this week. Falcon 9 completed its 650th flight on the Starlink 10-54

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:38.120
mission from Cape Canaveral. The mission carried 29 satellites and pushed the constellation total past

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:45.640
10,600. Block 5 boosters have now flown past their original design life, demonstrating the annual

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:51.980
launch rate headroom the company continues to test. The same orbital infrastructure Starlink is building

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:59.540
is also becoming a new compute platform. SpaceX unveiled the AI-1 Orbital Data Center satellite this week.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.500
The project targets one gigawatt of space compute capacity by late 2027.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:10.520
Company statements emphasize that the effort centers on orbital infrastructure rather than any merger

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:16.940
with XAI. The real business case rests on providing compute resources in orbit separate from

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:22.760
GROC development. Reports noted that investors are factoring these data center ambitions into

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:29.680
the current $2 trillion valuation. The announcement positions SpaceX hardware as a platform for future

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:35.740
space-based processing loads. Observers are tracking whether the one gigawatt goal aligns with

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:41.980
Starship refueling timelines needed to deploy such systems. Back on Earth hardware that collectors

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:48.380
can touch is now available for pre-order. SpaceX opened pre-orders for a full-scale replica of

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:54.100
the Raptor engine bell that matches the outer geometry of the flight article including nozzle contour

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:59.980
and mounting flange. The replica is produced with the same additive and subtractive processes used

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:05.480
on actual flight hardware. While replicas go on display the real flight hardware is already

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:11.980
stacking for the next Starship test. Starship flight 13 preparations include booster 20 receiving

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:19.420
its full set of 33 engines and ship 40 moving toward a static fire test. Revised Artemis lunar lander

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:24.800
plans took shape this week according to Space News reporting. The updates involve adjustments

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:30.600
to NASA's human landing system architecture. Starship remains the baseline lander element under

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:36.060
the revised approach. Discussions have addressed timeline shifts and integration milestones with

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:42.040
the overall Artemis program. These changes occur against the backdrop of ongoing Starship flight

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:47.920
test and refueling work. The revisions could influence the schedule for the first crude lunar landing.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.780
Listeners following the Mars ambitions thread will note how lunar lander decisions feed into

00:04:53.780 --> 00:05:00.180
broader deep space architecture choices. An op-ed published this week examined the broader significance

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:05.660
of SpaceX within the aerospace sector. The piece highlighted how the company's engineering

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:11.780
progress has altered access to orbit. It connected Starship development and Starlink deployment to

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:17.900
downstream effects on national space programs. The commentary also referenced public market value

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:24.140
milestones reached after the SNP-CX listing. Readers were directed to consider the long-term

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:29.660
implications for both commercial and government missions. The op-ed appeared alongside coverage

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:35.940
of the $2 trillion valuation driven by Starship and Orbital Compute Bets. Before we go,

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:41.440
that static fire attempt and the engine install on booster 20 are the items to watch next week.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:48.940
And that's a wrap on today's SpaceX developments. SPCX closed at $160.95.

